include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose, and the point is WHICH refusal.
--
-- `leave` and `repeat` never consult the definition table: they resolve against
-- the scopes actually open at that point, and exactly three things get onto
-- that list -- a named scope or loop, a `likely` road, a `when` road -- plus a
-- spliced template body. A definition never runs, so it can never be on it.
--
-- That was already true, but the message said only *'linux.file' is not a scope
-- this sits inside (open here: none)*, and then explained at length why a jump
-- must target an ancestor. All true, and none of it about the actual mistake,
-- which is that the name denotes a thing with no body at all.
--
-- The reading the language settled on is the one to hand back: `is` declares and
-- `goes` runs, so an `is` has nothing to leave. Four kinds of name get their own
-- sentence -- a namespace, a definition, an instance, a primitive -- and a name
-- that denotes nothing still falls through to the general message, which is the
-- right one when there is no better answer.
--
--   refused:  'file' is a definition -- it declares, and only a `goes` body runs

program goes
  n is 0

  leave file

  ensure n == 0
end
